you  hello and welcome to my talk on  improving low connectivity experiences  on the web I'm Valerie Willard  Srinivasan I work at panoply the podcast  network so yes podcasts if you want  podcast recommendations definitely catch  me in the hallway later but for now I  want to talk a little bit about the  internet also I have my twitter handle  up here  valerie codes feel free to tweet me your  thoughts favorite quotes favorite  podcasts whatever i'd love to hear about  hear from you but first let's talk a  little bit about the internet I love the  internet we all probably do I'm willing  to guess that most of us here have spent  plenty of time they're given the topics  of this conference and are used to  putting it to use for many aspects of  our personal and professional lives this  is one of my favorite things from the  internet it's an owl with a tiny Santa  hat on but the Internet is not just a  great place for finding cute owls with  Santa hats it's an incredibly important  tool we're all fans of the internet and  work with it every day we've seen the  power and impact that our work can have  and wouldn't it be great if that were  available to more people but while I'm  willing to bet that pretty much everyone  in this room has a reliable high-speed  Internet connection at home or at work  or let's be honest probably both that's  far from the case for the rest of the  world 54% of households worldwide have  internet access 84% of households in  developed countries and only 15% of  households in least developed countries  have access to the Internet some of  these users might only be able to get  online from a mobile phone network or  from their local library so perhaps it  goes without saying the more forgiving  your applications or websites are two  people who have slow or limited in  internet access the more people will be  able to use them opening up additional  possibilities for viewers or customers  from around the globe  it's also worth noting the regional and  economic variations in internet  accessibility and keeping in mind that  internet speed could be a hurdle to  people in lower income brackets or from  rural areas using your site this makes  me think of this quote by William Gibson  the future is already here it's just not  very evenly distributed as well as being  the home for almost all of the work that  we as developers do the Internet has a  lot of promise to level disparities  caused by difference in class education  and access in many ways it is the future  but just like the future the Internet is  not evenly distributed either we saw on  the last slide that there are huge  differences in countries internet access  due to wealth wealth and infrastructure  in addition to that rural households are  less likely to have internet access  poorer families are less likely to have  internet access and more likely to only  access the internet on a smartphone  rather than a computer people with more  education are more likely to access the  Internet men are more likely than women  young people are more likely than older  people so while there may be not much we  can do for users who never access the  internet ever at all there are lots of  ways that we can help users who may have  limited internet access and there are  lots of ways that access can be limited  let's talk about the different types of  ways people can be internet limited  people may fall into multiple of these  categories and they might may not be  permanent or apply to all of the ways in  which a person accesses the Internet  first there's the intermittent  connection we've all probably  experienced this as we try to use our  mobile data network as we're on the  train or going between service areas  this could also be a fixed Internet  access point that is unreliable due to  service providers or whether in these  cases the phone or computer may even  report that is it's connected to a  network  but packets are not actually being  delivered  next there's the slow connection where a  reliable connection exists but it may be  much slower than what we're used to  making it difficult to load websites  with lots of rich content or background  scripts there's also the  bandwidth-limited connection which again  we're probably much more familiar with  on our phone but also exists on home  internet plans some users may be limited  to to a certain number of gigabytes of  data data use a month making them have a  much higher awareness of how much data  they're actually using some people may  also have sporadic or time limited  connections which I've differentiated  from internet Mitton connections because  they may be able to predict that they'll  only be able to access the internet from  their their local library at a certain  time or a person who only connects to  the Internet in a given time frame and  may want to cache or save content for  later this is the part where I embarrass  my mom I also made her take this picture  for me it is of her internet satellite  dish as you might guess she and my dad  lack access to high-speed Internet there  are no fibre or DSL lines up here what  there is is a clear view of the southern  sky so they get satellite internet with  a top speed of 5 megabits per second for  probably more than some of you pay for  your much cozier plans to top it off  they also have bandwidth caps that limit  their totally data usage to 10 gigabytes  a month and at the time this picture was  taken as may be evident they didn't have  any internet access at all because their  internet satellite dish was covered in  snow during the winter one of my parents  has to go out and periodically clean it  off if they want to keep using the  Internet here's a full list of the  internet plans available for from the my  parents internet provider you'll notice  that the top download speed is 5  megabits per second which is much lower  than the 25 megabits per second required  for that  CiCi's classification of high-speed  you'll also notice that all of the plans  have fairly low bandwidth limits if you  consider that streaming a movie can  easily consume a gigabyte of data but  there's unlimited data usage from  midnight to 5:00 a.m. so you can get all  your downloading done then the  limitations of this plan fall into three  of the categories that I discussed  earlier it's slower than we're all used  to its bandwidth limited and it's time  limited in that it's advantageous to get  all your data transfers done in the  unlimited window with these complaint  constraints in mind let's continue  problems are of limited connectivity are  not just limited to people in rural  areas here's a tweet from just a few  days ago from someone visiting Germany  who had limited data speeds  I think Nicole is actually at this  conference and this is from a story that  the New York Times did in August of 2015  entitled the costs of mobile ads on 50  news sites it was focusing on the cost  of loading these sites to mobile users  both in terms of little literal cost on  their phone plans and time spent waiting  for things to load but as we've seen a  lot of the challenges that apply to  mobile internet usage are also relevant  to other limited connectivity users it  found that most new sites took longer to  load ad content than editorial content  in some egregious examples up to three  times longer that means that you're  waiting three times longer time for your  ads to load then for the actual article  that you wanted to read given that these  sites rely heavily on advertising for  their revenue it doesn't seem like a  great idea for their ad scripts or  content to reduce speed to such a level  that people are incentivized to install  ad blockers this is an important point  to consider also as we consider  advertising within our projects and the  larger question  whether the costs to the user of viewing  or using our content is being spent in  loading content that is integral to what  the app does or whether it is added  features fun or polish if the things  that we're making users load aren't  essential are we giving them a way to  opt out just to illustrate this point a  little further with another recent  example USA today's European version of  their site got rid of tracking scripts  to comply with GDP are as a result the  size of an individual page went from  five megabytes to just 500 kilobytes  some users don't mind advertising but  they shouldn't have to sacrifice  performance in order to use to load ads  so where does that leave us  one of the most important things that  you can do to support these types of  users is to build responsive websites  more and more users are accessing the  internet from mobile phones and for many  especially lower-income people that is  the only way they access the Internet in  addition to being tolerant to flaky  networks as people move in and out of  coverage areas any website or app  serving these users needs to be  responsive and performed properly on a  small screen and possibly underpowered  device another important thing we can do  is just communicate signal and allow  people to opt out of high bandwidth  portions of your site for example from  both a bandwidth and a user experience  perspective there's really no reason for  you to have an auto playing video on  your site users should know any time  they're about to download a file you can  use little download symbols and indicate  the file size if possible it should be  clear to users when they're about to  start playing a video or stream which  again should never autoplay think about  how you structure your content remember  that sites are loaded top to bottom so  by putting important things at the  beginning of your HTML document  those things will load first allowing  users with slow internet to have  something to read before all of the  content finishes loading place large  assets or Java scripts toward the end if  it's not essential that baby loaded as  soon as the page renders I had to  include this because I was adapting this  talk for a European audience and I know  you like soccer I like animals this  combines the two talking about headers  optimize your caching by getting  familiar with what cache headers you're  setting and trying to avoid users having  to refetch content if it hasn't changed  things like fonts style sheets other  static assets are all examples of things  that probably don't change much over  time and you can improve your users  experience by not forcing a reload every  time here are some guidelines based on  UX research and attention it takes about  one second for users to notice that  they're waiting for something to load  and start getting impatient it's ideal  to have at least some content loaded  after two seconds even if you can't load  the entire page in that time frame on a  slow connection it'll at least give the  user something to do while they wait  after 10 seconds a user will start to  lose interest and is likely to be  diverted by another task it should take  one minute or less for a user to  complete a simple task on your app like  signing up or making a purchase longer  than that and users will start to  perceive sluggishness so you can try  testing some of these guidelines and  simulating a degraded Network  environment and see how long they take  on your sites  I found this recommendation from a UK  NGO from about 10 years ago that  recommended an upper limit on page size  of 25 kilobytes this was based on the  connection speeds of universities in  Africa and the rationale that such a  page would take 10 seconds to load over  a 20  kilobit per second connection but as a  lot of you probably know this is really  small this may not be a realistic  guideline for a lot of people it's older  and the infrastructure may have improved  but at the same time limiting page size  is very important in light of that  recommendation let's look at some of the  common libraries that we're probably all  using jQuery clocks in at 85 kilobytes  bootstraps j/s is 50 kilobytes whereas  it's CSS is another 125 d3 is 216  kilobytes of course these are necessary  parts of lots of sites for various  reasons but it's worth being aware of  the cost of these inclusions especially  as we consider that these are all before  we get to the size of any of the actual  content on your web site and the cost of  including these libraries for  bandwidth-limited users if you're using  jQuery for a couple functions that are  possible but a little ugly or without it  maybe consider making that sacrifice if  you'd like to check the sizes of pages  yourself the network tab on chrome dev  tools can help with profiling us if you  disable the cache and load a page it'll  give you the total size of data  transferred and of course with caching  this is probably not the amount of data  users actually have to load on every  page load but it can still give you some  idea I tried measuring the size of a  couple pages in chrome dev tools they  were all at least a couple hundred  kilobytes and plenty went well into the  megabytes Google's PageSpeed insights is  another great resource you can take a  URL and it'll give suggestions for how  to make that page faster so the ideas  we've talked about so far are helpful  for people with slow internet  connections or limited bandwidth but  what about the separate issue of an  unreliable internet connection or one  that says it's connected but isn't  there's a growing movement toward  offline first development which assumes  that a user does not have connection and  serves from a cache and if there is  connection updated information can be  fetched and rendered of course it's not  magic and only works if the user has  successfully connected before in order  to populate the cache but at least it  does not assume that they need a  reliable connection every single time  email clients are probably the sorts of  applications that were most familiar  with it used this paradigm even if you  aren't connected you can see the  messages downloaded from the last time  you were you can even write messages  that are queued up to be sent next time  you have a connection paradigm is part  of progressive web app development and  can also be applied to your slick sites  and web apps  the idea of offline first is especially  helpful to mobile users and others who  might not be able to predict when  they'll be offline adding additional UI  signals like google drives make  available offline can aid in those  scenarios with some research you'll find  lots of resources around offline first  and how to start using it  I recommend the article designing  offline first web apps on a list apart  as a primer on the topic and some of the  best practices and ideas around it as  well as resources on how to contribute  your ideas and learn more the big idea  is that being offline is normal and  something that should be treated as such  rather than just throwing an error or  exception a serviceworker is now  supported on all major browsers it runs  as soon as the page is requested and  before that request is sent out to the  network and conserve from a cache if the  network is unavailable serviceworker  introduces a new storage API a place to  store responses keyed by requests  similar to the browser cache but it  gives you a lot more control over what  and how content is cached then a simple  browser header this can allow you to  tell browsers to cache your pages and  content locally so that if a user loses  internet connection they can still  access your site  as you set up a serviceworker you  actually have a lot of control over how  it works  so you could have it display a custom  your offline page similar to a 404 page  or you can get more creative by having  it cash the last couple articles that a  user has read on your site or maybe the  most common content that people come to  your site for you can also put use this  to put control back into the users hands  by giving them some sort of UI option to  save something for online use or for  offline use here's Google Chrome's  default online page you can play a  little game with the tier X which makes  it somewhat less frustrating another  useful tool in Google Chrome is that you  can simulate different network  conditions in developer tools so you can  test how your website or what web app  looks and performs to users on different  types of connections so before we part  ways I want to talk a bit more about why  I think this topic is important this  quote is from a story on limit limited  connectivity users primarily those using  only a phone and their access to news  sites as you can imagine as capabilities  improve to make the most complex  infographics and most flashy and  interactive features keep in mind the  users that you may be leaving behind as  you make those choices it's really easy  to always want to be on the cutting edge  always working in the coolest technology  but we have to remember that that's all  privileged in many places that's a quote  from era Aaron Williams a graphics  reporter at the Washington Post from a  pure business perspective the best way  to reach more customers is to make your  product available and useful to more  people if your product website or app  has a billion possible users chances are  good that some of them will be in the  groups we talked about earlier who have  limited internet access be that due to  Geographic limitations economic  limitations or some combination of the  two  it's also worth remembering that  constant and reliable internet  connectivity is a privilege and by  leaving out those who don't have it  we're leaving out a specific segment of  the population largely rural and or low  income in the last year in the United  States we've been involved in a very  heated exchange around the Internet's  role in shaping our country our politics  and our policies as we come to rely on  the internet more and more in our daily  lives both in our logistics and our  decision-making we need to be aware of  the consequences that making our tools  and information inaccessible to low  connectivity users has access to  legitimate news sources was a huge issue  in the last u.s. election and one that  is likely to continue to loom large as  time goes on we look specifically here  at news sites and the toll that  advertising scripts can take on low  connectivity users if those users aren't  able to load legitimate news sites then  we're in trouble  if services applications or tools are  similarly unavailable to that group we  have a huge problem think about this and  your responsibilities in this system as  you develop websites and design tools  for the future I hope that this has  given you some more context and tools  for supporting and empathizing with low  connectivity users in your development  process if nothing else I hope that I've  convinced you to never add an auto  playing video to your website I'd love  to hear more from you my twitter handle  is valerie codes and my email is Valerie  Willard at gmail.com again my name is  Valerie Willard Srinivasan if you're  interested in podcast programming and  living in the Washington DC area I'd  love to talk more about roles at panoply  or we can just talk about our favorite  podcasts thank you again for having me  I'll be posting these slides shortly on  my Twitter feed and including links to  citations and more resources

